sks to propose.\n\nLooking at the use cases and their corresponding PRDs, I need to pick just one task following the dependency ordering principle—foundational libraries before features. Since pkg/testutils/ is the most foundational (all differential tests depend on it) and already has implementation, writing tests for it would provide the most value. Actually, wait. Let me think about what's actually needed. The packages exist but have no tests. The use cases are about getting these packages fully working. Since the constraint is 1 task max, let me pick the most impactful.\n\nFollowing P8 (deterministic ordering): libraries and shared packages before consumers. Among the three packages, pkg/testutils/ is the testing infrastructure that everything else depends on.\n\nLet me propose tests for pkg/testutils/ since:\n1. It's the most foundational (all other utilities' tests depend on it)\n2. The code exists but tests don't\n3. It's in release 00.0 (uc004-testutils)\n\nI need to anchor to specific PRD requirements. The PRD is prd001-testutils. Looking at the source code, I can see the requirements referenced across the full API surface: the DiffTest struct and its configuration options like stdin handling and environment setup, the core RunDiffTests function and its supporting helpers like runBinary and timeout configuration, the comparison logic that validates stdout, stderr, and exit codes with divergence reporting, the normalizer system including TimestampNormalizer and ComposeNormalizers for flexible output matching, and the file handling capabilities for expected outputs. - R5.2: File content comparison\n\nThe implementation already covers R1-R5. What's missing is tests.\n\nPer P2: \"Always split implementation and tests into separate tasks.\" Since implementation exists, I should propose the test task.\n\nLet me write a test task for pkg/testutils/ - testing the differential testing harness itself. I need to be careful about what requirements to reference. For the test task, I should target the key behaviors: RunDiffTests running both binaries and comparing outputs, divergence reporting, normalization hooks, and TimestampNormalizer - that's 4 requirements which fits the constraint.\n\nTesting the test harness itself is a bit meta. I'd need mock binaries or simple real binaries like `echo` or `cat` to verify that RunDiffTests correctly identifies matching outputs, identifies divergent outputs, and that normalization functions work properly. The test file would follow the naming convention and mirror the source structure.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering whether testutils is the most impactful next step. Looking at the use cases, I have three library packages with implementations but no tests - format, sys, and testutils - plus cmd/version from the magefiles use case. Following the principle of ordering types before implementations and libraries before consumers, testutils is the most foundational since it supports testing the others. But cmd/version is also a valid candidate as a consumer package.\n\nSince I need to anchor to specific PRD requirements and can only include at most 4, I'm selecting the most critical testable areas: verifying that RunDiffTests executes both binaries and compares outputs, ensuring divergence is reported with the necessary context, confirming that normalization hooks apply to both outputs, and validating that the TimestampNormalizer correctly replaces timestamp patterns.\n\nNow I'm drafting the actual task specification with the YAML structure that defines the deliverable, including the required reading materials, the test file to create, and the specific requirements that need to be validated. R2.1): verify that RunDiffTests executes both binaries with identical args, stdin, and env, and passes when outputs match\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Test divergence reporting (prd001 R3.5): verify that RunDiffTests fails the test and reports args, stdin, ref stdout/stderr/exit, and go stdout/stderr/exit when outputs diverge\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Test normalization (prd001 R4.1, R4.3): verify that NormalizeFunc hooks are applied to both binaries' stdout and stderr before comparison, and that multiple normalizers compose in order\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Test TimestampNormalizer (prd001 R4.2): verify that TimestampNormalizer replaces 'Mon DD HH:MM:SS', 'YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS', and standalone 'HH:MM:SS' patterns with the placeholder string\"\n\nFor the design approach, I'm using standard Unix utilities like /bin/echo and /bin/cat as test binaries to keep things portable across Darwin and Linux without needing compiled helpers. I'll generate divergent outputs using shell commands to simulate different behavior between the reference and Go implementations. The timestamp normalizer tests will use table-driven test cases covering all three timestamp patterns. The acceptance criteria is straightforward—the test suite needs to pass completely with no failures. I'm realizing I shouldn't invent design decisions that aren't grounded in the PRD—the constraint is clear about that. But I need to give the stitch agent practical guidance on how to test the test harness without making it overly complex. Looking at the PRD references in the source code, I can see what requirements are actually specified: the DiffTest struct, RunDiffTests function, binary execution with timeouts, working directory handling, environment setup, and the comparison and normalization logic. The PRD likely already defines the testing approach, so I should derive my design decisions from what's actually documented there rather than inventing implementation details. files. The stitch agent can figure this out from the required_reading.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering whether testutils is really the right starting point—testing the test framework itself is somewhat meta. The alternative would be straightforward unit tests for pkg/sys/ or pkg/format/, but P5 and P8 make it clear that testutils is foundational since all other tests depend on it, so it should be validated first.\n\nLet me think through the ordering more carefully against P8's principle of \"libraries before consumers, implementation before tests.\" All three libraries have implementations, so the question is which tests to prioritize. testutils has no dependencies on the others, sys doesn't depend on format or testutils, and format's color.go has its own isTerminal implementation that should probably use pkg/sys instead.\n\nSince testutils is the foundational testing infrastructure, its tests should come first—that's the right call. I'll propose just this one task given the max 1 constraint, and I'm estimating the test file should land around 150-250 lines with comprehensive coverage of the main test cases.\n\nNow I need to be careful about what goes into design_decisions—only concrete choices from the PRD itself, not invented ones. Let me re-examine what's actually specified versus what I'm deriving from the architecture.\n\nThe test file naming follows the project convention from P7, and the tests will exercise the public API without modifying existing code. Those are the only design decisions I can confidently include since they're grounded in the documented patterns.\n\nNow I'm laying out the test structure: I need to create unit tests for RunDiffTests that verify it executes both binaries with matching arguments, stdin, and environment variables, then confirm the test passes when stdout, stderr, and exit codes all align. I'll also need to cover divergence scenarios and the normalization functions like TimestampNormalizer and ComposeNormalizers. ence detection (prd001-testutils R3.2, R3.3, R3.4, R3.5): verify that RunDiffTests fails the subtest when stdout diverges, when stderr diverges, or when exit codes differ\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Test normalization (prd001-testutils R4.1, R4.3, R4.4): verify that NormalizeFunc hooks in DiffTest.Normalize are applied to both binaries' output before comparison, and that ComposeNormalizers applies functions in order\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Test TimestampNormalizer (prd001-testutils R4.2): verify replacement of 'Mon DD HH:MM:SS', 'YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS', and standalone 'HH:MM:SS' patterns with the placeholder '<TIMESTAMP>'\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Tests exercise only the public API exported by pkg/testutils (RunDiffTests, TimestampNormalizer, ComposeNormalizers). No changes to difftest.go.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use sh -c as the binary path for both goBinary and refBinary arguments to RunDiffTests. This avoids compiling test fixture binaries while still exercising the full execution path (fork, exec, capture stdout/stderr/exit code).\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"go test ./pkg/testutils/... passes with no failures\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Tests cover: matching outputs (pass), divergent stdout, divergent stderr, divergent exit code\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"All requirements R1-R4 are verified by at least one test case\"\n\nNow I'm laying out the design approach: I'll only test the public API surface without modifying the internal difftest implementation, and I'll use shell commands as test binaries to avoid needing to compile fixtures while still exercising the full execution and capture flow. The acceptance criteria confirm that the test suite passes cleanly and covers all the key scenarios including matching outputs and various divergence cases. implementation detail that might be necessary for the stitch agent to execute properly, so I'll frame it as practical guidance grounded in the testutils architecture rather than an arbitrary choice. Let me also double-check the task structure: four requirements, two design decisions, and four acceptance criteria fit within the constraints, and a single modified file is well below the 5-7 file limit. I'm noticing a tension between the general guidance asking for 5-8 requirements and the specific constraint capping it at 4, so the constraint takes precedence here.\n\nThe title should anchor to the requirement IDs as specified, so I'm including the PRD references and the specific requirements being addressed. I should also verify that the required_reading section points to any existing test suite documentation relevant to this work. I'm structuring the test file with required reading from the product requirements and test suite specifications, then laying out the test cases for RunDiffTests covering the key requirements around divergence detection, normalization, and the TimestampNormalizer functionality. I need to verify that TimestampNormalizer correctly replaces various timestamp patterns like 'Mon DD HH:MM:SS', ISO format, and standalone times with a placeholder. I'm also laying out the design decisions around testing only the public API without modifying the source, and using shell commands as test binaries to avoid compiling fixtures. The acceptance criteria are starting to define what needs to pass.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the test suite file naming convention — it should follow the pattern docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-[release-id].yaml, so for release 00.0 that would be test-rel-00.0.yaml.\n\nI should also include the use case document at docs/specs/use-cases/rel00.0-uc004-testutils.yaml to give the stitch agent full context. But looking at what's actually most useful for the agent, I want to keep the required_reading focused on the PRD as the primary reference, the test suite for specific test cases, and the source code being tested.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":46346,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0abb